From 7eea4bf1c57ed91e0ae12500e300c2a2e814fbeb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:29:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: say when a fixed refresh rate keeps Dolby
 Vision from native output

With the refresh rate not adjusted to the video, and the GUI at a rate that the transport does
not have (100, 119.88, 120 or 144 Hz, or 48 Hz), native output is not used, which is right:
Dolby Vision never takes another refresh rate than the one the video gets. The player
information said "the output mode is not 3840x2160 at 24-60 Hz", which reads as if the mode had
the wrong size. It now says that the refresh rate is fixed outside 24-60 Hz (reason "refresh"),
and the log says what to change: turn on "Adjust display refresh rate" (Settings > Player >
Videos). DVBRIDGE::RefreshRateKeepsOrdinary() decides it; a choice of output that the user made
for the video is said in its place, as for the mode.

A host test checks it at the rates of the transport and at 48, 100, 119.88, 120 and 144 Hz, with
the refresh rate fixed or adjusted, and in an interlaced or 3D mode; two mutations make it fail.

On top of the reasons of "windowing/gbm: say why native Dolby Vision is
not used": the fixed refresh rate is the reason only where the output
offers a mode that carries native output (OffersDVTransportMode(), which
tells "nomode" too). An output that offers none (an HDMI 1.4 port,
cable, adapter or TV input) still says "nomode", which turning on
"Adjust display refresh rate" would not change. On top of "VideoPlayer:
Dolby Vision that VAAPI does not decode says why; DRM PRIME leaves what
it cannot decode": the reason of the decoder (vaapioff, vaapicodec,
software) takes the place of "refresh" as it takes that of "mode", since
the pictures cannot be drawn natively anyway.
